Formatting commentary with Claude...

  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Energy leads the tape into month-end. Brent (+1.4% to 90.31) and WTI (+1.7% to 85.00) pushed higher as the Hormuz crisis entered a new phase, with reports that Washington won't tap the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to ease fuel prices — removing a key supply backstop just as the Caspian Pipeline Consortium decides later Friday whether to halt oil operations. The distillate complex followed (heating oil +1.6%, gasoil +1.4%), TTF gas jumped 3.1%, and EUAs rose in sympathy; RBOB's 2.3% slide was the outlier. The supply-risk premium is real and the desk should watch that CPC decision closely — a halt would tighten seaborne flows further.

Risk tone was otherwise constructive, powered by a chip-sector rebound: Nasdaq futures rose 1.1% as strong hyperscaler results — notably Amazon's cloud beat — reignited the AI trade and Korean chipmakers logged a historic rally, driving KOSPI strength and a 1.4% KRW pop. The bigger question this earnings season is whether cheaper AI models erode the extraordinary profits underpinning index-level valuations — watch capex commentary, not just beats. Rates offered no support: Treasuries slipped across the curve with yields near long-term highs after the Fed held, and the BoJ's hawkish hold adds to the global tightening tone ahead of the US jobs report.

The dollar snapped a five-day losing run as the yen's intervention-driven rally stalled (JPY -0.8%), knocking precious metals hard — gold -1.3%, silver -1.8%, PGMs off similarly — a positioning washout rather than a change in the structural bid.

